session: TA 0.9 critical-enablement — new Type-3 plan kickoff

## What this is

A kickoff for a **brand-new Type-3 coding plan** whose job is to fix **all critical
ThroughputAnalyzer (TA) enablement issues for the 0.9 release**. Dean's framing (2026-08-04):
the planner needs to understand *how to change the current code* (as mapped in
`planning/multi-analyzer-dataflow-map.md`) to make TA actually usable — i.e. the multi-analyzer
engine correctly builds and binds the anchor, non-saturation analyzers can be enabled and bind,
and the failure/liveness policy is sound. The design discussion that produced the corrected
mechanism is now written up as an explicit **spec** in Part 2 of
`planning/ta-anchor-refactor-review.md`. Use that as the authoritative spec; use the map doc to
ground every change in the actual current code.

**This kickoff SUPERSEDES the earlier `plan__ta-anchor-refactor-mechanism-redesign.md` handoff**
(still `.WIP` in this dir). Two reasons it is superseded, not merely amended:
1. **Different deliverable.** The earlier handoff asked you to *fold the correction into the
   existing `ta-anchor-refactor-plan.md`*. Dean's current direction is to author a **new**
   Type-3 plan (new filename; likely a **fresh branch** — see "Framing" in review §Part 2 intro).
   Treat `ta-anchor-refactor-plan.md` as **Part 1 material only** (a superseded plan under review),
   not the doc to edit.
2. **Stale section refs.** That handoff points at a `"Design correction (2026-08-04)"` section at
   `L490-784`. That section no longer exists — the review doc was **restructured** into Part 1
   (review of the old plan) and Part 2 (the spec). The correct pointers are below.

If you already began folding into `ta-anchor-refactor-plan.md` under the old handoff: stop editing
that plan doc, mark `plan__ta-anchor-refactor-mechanism-redesign.md.WIP` → `.DONE`, and treat this
kickoff as the live task. Nothing is lost — all the content moved into review §Part 2.

## The two source documents (read in this order)

1. **`planning/ta-anchor-refactor-review.md` → Part 2** (`# Part 2 — Recommended course of change:
   spec for the new Type-3 plan`, starts **L526**). This is the spec. Section map:
   - **§2.1 The corrected two-phase mechanism** (L552) — Phase 1 (generation: call every analyzer
     incl. sat, tag each ballot entry with enablement+error status, **order-independent**, no
     decisions) and Phase 2 (the binding/anchor getter — successor to `saturationEntry`: check
     statuses → find binding analyzer → build `anchor.VariantCapacities` **merged per variant by
     `VariantName`**, `(a)` identity fields from sat, `(b)` sizing fields from binding analyzer;
     **per-variant fallback to sat's `(b)` runs BEFORE disabled-entry removal**; **no stored
     `.Anchor` field** — derived on demand; no analyzer-level sort).
   - **§2.2 How this redesign resolves the Part-1 findings** (L592) — maps E1/E3 resolved,
     E2 likely-moot (no stored field ⇒ possibly no 87-site `withSatEntry` fix), E4/E5 eased,
     F1→QM-error, etc. Read this to know which Part-1 findings you no longer need to design around.
   - **§2.3 Decisions (surrounding the mechanism)** (L629) — (1) QM = **error/DEFERRED** (refuse to
     dispatch, never silent sat-v2 fallback); (2) liveness "no live analyzers → do nothing" reusing
     `emitSafetyNetMetrics`; (3) `AnalyzerName` validation gap = **separate small PR**, not this
     plan; (4) scale-from-zero engine is **orthogonal**.
   - **§2.4 Partial scale-from-zero under `[TA]`-alone** (L686) — **STILL UNDER DISCUSSION**
     ("almost agree," not final). Rely on `scalefromzero` as the interim reactive net; Mechanism
     A/B is not locked. **Do not commit this to a hard plan commit** — carry it as an open
     design question / deferred item until Dean finalizes.
   - **§2.5 TA-side proactive complement** (L778) — **agreed**. Persist `lastPerReplicaSupply`
     *and* `Cost`/`AcceleratorName` together on `variantState` (same eviction window as
     `lastFittedB`); the Go snippet is in the section. **Ranking-inversion guard is mandatory**:
     defaulting only PRC while leaving `Cost=0` makes `costEfficiency = 0/PRC = 0`, ranking the
     variant *cheapest* — both fields must fall back together.
   - **§2.6 Test requirements for the new plan** (L866) — 8 numbered test obligations; each new
     plan commit should map to one or more.
   - **§2.7 Scope boundaries & deferred items** (L909) — the fences: in-scope / under-discussion /
     separate-PR / deferred-to-PR-2 / orthogonal. **Honor these when scoping commits** so the plan
     does not swallow the under-discussion (§2.4) or separate-PR (§2.3-3) items.

2. **`planning/multi-analyzer-dataflow-map.md`** — the current-code map. Ground every "change X"
   in the plan against the real symbol/file/line here (esp. §8 Dean's review annotations).
   Companion: `planning/scale-from-to-zero-analysis.md` (scale-from/to-zero mechanisms; relevant
   only to §2.4's interim reliance on `scalefromzero`).

**Part 1 of the review doc is NOT a task list.** It reviews the superseded plan (F1–F12/C1/E1–E13)
purely so its findings are on record and Part 2 can cite "this is now resolved." The blockquote at
the top of Part 1 says it explicitly: do not implement against Part 1's per-finding
"Fix (planner)" directions — Part 2 supersedes them.

## What to produce

1. A **new Type-3 plan doc** — you choose the filename (suggest `planning/ta-09-enablement-plan.md`
   or similar; not `ta-anchor-refactor-plan.md`, which is the superseded doc). Micro-rules format
   (Reading Protocol + `## TOC` with `L<start>:<end>` ranges + `[↑ TOC]` links); run
   `bash plans/scripts/toc-refresh.sh <newfile>` before you hand it off.
2. Per-commit structure implementing §2.1–§2.3 (and §2.5), each commit naming the exact
   file/symbol from the map doc it touches, and the dev-guide section(s) it must update
   (`docs/developer-guide/` — name them specifically, per CONVENTIONS; the map doc / current
   dev-guide will tell you which).
3. Explicit **deferred/under-discussion carve-outs** matching §2.7: keep §2.4 (partial
   scale-from-zero mechanism A/B) and §2.3-3 (`AnalyzerName` validation) OUT of the committed plan
   scope — record them as deferred items with design intent (per the deletion-documentation rule),
   don't design them into a commit.
4. The **semantic-pivot grep step** for any behavioral-contract change (e.g. `saturationEntry` →
   the new binding getter; QM silent-drop → QM-error): old search term + files to scan, per
   CONVENTIONS' plan-authoring rule.
5. A note on **branch handling** (fresh branch vs. reworking `ta-anchor-refactor@34055d77`) — your
   call per the branch-state analysis in the superseded handoff; whatever you decide, write it in
   the plan.

## How to signal me when the plan is ready (IMPORTANT — exact filename)

When the new plan doc is complete and toc-refreshed, drop a **ready-signal handoff at this exact
path** so my auto-review picks it up without colliding with the code-reviewer's or coder's channels:

```
session/handoffs/review__ta09-enablement-plan-ready.md
```

Body (three header lines + refs; this one is a genuine review request, so a short prose note is
fine):
```
to: review
reason: new-plan-ready
refs:
  - planning/<your-new-plan-filename>.md
  - planning/ta-anchor-refactor-review.md   (Part 2 = the spec this plan implements)
note: <one line — plan filename, branch decision, anything you want flagged for review>
```

I am watching for **that exact filename**. Do NOT reuse `review__ta-anchor-refactor-criteria.md`
(that's the code-reviewer's PR-1 push checklist, a different role) or any `ta-anchor-refactor__*`
coder trigger — those won't reach me and will confuse routing.

